[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/QU8o2AIu/QnW3X7S4"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.06"]
[UTCTime "15:33:29"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The Chigorin Variation is similar to the King's Indian Attack, and we can do basically the same thing against it. }
1. e4 { Let's play the French Defense. } 1... e6 2. Qe2 { This strange move is the Chigorin Variation. If we play 2. ... d5 we just lose a pawn to 3. exd5 because our e-pawn is pinned. Better is 2. ... c5 (you can play that on the board) } 2... c5 3. Nf3 { White wants to play a King's Indian Attack - like setup. Let's develop a knight. } 3... Nc6 4. g3 { What kingside setup did we play against the King's Indian Attack? It still works here! } 4... g6 5. Bg2 { And we develop our bishop... } 5... Bg7 6. O-O { And a knight.... } 6... Nge7 7. d3 { Now, we castle. } 7... O-O 8. c3 { We have a solid position, and have plans similar to the King's Indian Attack. } *

[Event "French Defense: Two Knights Variation"]
[Date "2021.11.06"]
