[ChapterName "Two Knights Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/QU8o2AIu/IZbgdDHB"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.06"]
[UTCTime "15:40:53"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The Two Knights is very different than our previous two lines. White remains flexible, not committing to any specific setup. }
1. e4 { You know what to do! } 1... e6 2. Nc3 { What's our usual counterattack? } 2... d5 3. Nf3 { This is the Two Knights. I recommend abandoning our usual French setups and just chasing white's knights. How? } 3... d4 4. Ne2 { And how do we support our pawn? } 4... c5 { Great job. We have equalized the position. We have more central and queenside space, but white can try to play pawn breaks like c3 and b4, and they have a lead in development. } *

