[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.06"]
[UTCTime "15:50:24"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ At last, we've come to the main lines of the French. First up is the favorite line of legendary players Garry Kasparov and Paul Morphy, who often played this line as white. }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 { What's our usual counterattack? } 2... d5 3. exd5 exd5 { This is the Exchange variation. This opening has gotten a reputation for being "drawish" and "boring," but then why would Kasparov and Morphy, both brilliant attackers, play it? Well, they thought white's extra tempo was enough to give them a bit of an advantage. 4. Nf3 is the only critical move, favored by both of these brilliant players. } 4. Nf3 { Just continue symmetrically... } 4... Nf6 5. Bd3 { Keep playing symmetrically. } 5... Bd6 6. O-O { OK, you can probably guess what you're going to do. } 6... O-O 7. Nc3 { Wait! Don't play 7. ... Nc6! Support your pawn with 7. ... c6. } { [%csl Gc6,Yd5][%cal Gc7c6,Yc6d5] } 7... c6 8. Bg5 { This was Morphy's favorite line, but we are actually doing fine here. We have a solid position that white won't be able to break through, with best play from both sides. } *

[Event "French Defense: Advance Variation"]
[Date "2021.11.06"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C02"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Main Line"]
[StudyName "French Defense"]
[ChapterName "Advance Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/QU8o2AIu/x8YEvLdL"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.06"]
[UTCTime "16:05:10"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ In this chapter, we're looking at the Advance Variation, a popular and aggressive way to play against the French. }
1. e4 { ... } 1... e6 2. d4 { Do you remember what to do here? } 2... d5 3. e5 { This is why it's called the Advance Variation. White advances their pawn! How should we counterattack? } 3... c5 4. c3 { Which piece should we develop? } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 { Here, we should play 5. ... Qb6, to continue to pressure d4 but also to put a bit of pressure on b2. } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd8b6] } 5... Qb6 { White has 3 moves here:
- 6. Bd3 doesn't hang a pawn as 6. ... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nxd4?? 8. Nxd4 Qxd4?? 9. Bb5+ black's queen is lost, but just 6. ... cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 white will lose a tempo with 8. Be2 or 8. Bc2 or a pawn with 8. 0-0.
- 6. Be2 is a slightly weak move, as 6. ... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nh6 followed by ... Nf5 gives us lots of pressure on the d4-pawn
- 6. a3 is the main line. } { [%csl Be2,Yd3,Ga3][%cal Bf1e2,Yf1d3,Ga2a3] } 6. a3 { White aims to play b4, which attacks c5, stops all pressure on b2, gives the dark-squared bishop a new square on b2, and gains space. The critical move is 6. ... c4! } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc5c4] } 6... c4 { Yes, this move is actually good. All pressure on d4 is stopped, but now we aim to control b3. We threaten Nc6-a5-b3 when we will be better, so white has to stop this threat. One popular move is 7. Nbd2. } 7. Nbd2 { Where would our own knight like to go? } 7... Na5 { The threat of ...Nb3 remains, and will remain, for a long time. We are keeping white's queenside locked up, because if the d2-knight moves, we can play ... Nb3. If white does something other than 7. Nbd2, they have 2 options
- 7. b4 cxb3 e. p. (don't forget about en passant!) 9. Bb2 followed by 10. c4, the game is sharp. Who will seize the initiative?
-7. kingside development (g3 for example) we will play ... f6 at some point, which will likely lead to some complications as well.
That's it for the Advance Variation! But next, we're moving on to see what we can play against the main line. } *

[Event "French Defense: Rubinstein Variation"]
[Date "2021.11.06"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C10"]
[Opening "French Defense: Rubinstein Variation, Blackburne Defense"]
[StudyName "French Defense"]
[ChapterName "Rubinstein Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/QU8o2AIu/kDn30SSV"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.06"]
[UTCTime "16:30:01"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Other than the Exchange and Advance variations, white has two other moves in the main line. But, we can play the same thing against both of them! }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 { The two moves we haven't looked at yet are 3. Nc3, the Main Line, and 3. Nd2, the Tarrasch Variation. But we can play the same thing against each of them. } { [%csl Gc3,Bd2][%cal Gb1c3,Bb1d2] } 3. Nc3 { The blue arrows show some replies to the Main Line, the yellow arrows show some replies to the Tarrasch. Yes, 3. ... Nf6 can be played against each of them, but it's not our main move. Our move is 3. ... dxe4, the Rubinstein Variation. } { [%csl Bb4,Yc5,Yf6,Ge4][%cal Bf8b4,Yc7c5,Bg8f6,Gd5e4] } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 { Black has no weaknesses, and can play ... c5 to have the only central pawn left. But first, we need to develop. } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 4... Nd7 5. Nf3 { Where should our other knight go? } 5... Ngf6 { White has a few options:
- 6. Ng3?! c5 when we have a good game, we've accomplished our goal without making any concessions.
- 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 Be7 when we have a solid position, and ... c5 is still our goal
-6. Nxf6+ is the main line. } { [%csl Rg3,Bg5,Gf6][%cal Re4g3,Bc1g5,Ge4f6] } 6. Nxf6+ { How should we recapture? } 6... Nxf6 { Once again, there are 3 main options.
- 7. c3 c5 8. Ne5 white may end up with a weak d-pawn, but has active play. 8. ... a6, preventing Bb5+, then ... cxd4 is best.
- 7. Bg5 c5 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Bxd7 Qxd7, when we don't have to worry about 10. Bxf6 with doubled f-pawns, practice has shown. 10. Qe2, followed by 0-0-0 is another possibility.
- 7. Bd3 is the main line. } { [%csl Bg5,Yc3,Gd3][%cal Bc1g5,Yc2c3,Gf1d3] } 7. Bd3 { What's our main idea in the Rubinstein? } 7... c5 8. dxc5 { Recapture... } 8... Bxc5 { Now, white has another decision: which way to castle?
- 9. Qe2 0-0 10. Bg5 or 10. Bd2 followed by 0-0-0 when the game is about equal, black has an extra central pawn and a kingside pawn majority, and white has control over open files and a queenside pawn majority
- 9. 0-0 is the most popular. } { [%csl Gg1,Be2][%cal Bd1e2,Ge1g1] } 9. O-O { We can castle, too. } 9... O-O 10. Bg5 { Let's prepare to bring a bishop to the long diagonal. } 10... b6 11. Qe2 { Let's develop our bishop (finally!) } 11... Bb7 12. Rad1 { Threatening 13. Bxh7+! winning the queen. So, move our queen. } { [%cal Rd1d8] } 12... Qc7 { Once again, we have an extra central pawn and a kingside pawn majority, but white has a queenside pawn majority and control over the open file. } *

